{"elapsed_seconds": 9.3529252560038, "event": {"type": "chat.end", "result": {"model_instance_id": "evo-accuracy-qwen35-off-mtp", "output": [{"type": "message", "content": "The author describes the Blues and Greens as factions that evolved from circus groups into dangerous political parties by 532 [PDF p. 101]. Their unrest began when rioters demanded the execution of leaders, but a botched hanging allowed two criminals to escape into sanctuary [PDF p. 102]. This incident triggered six days of rioting where the factions united under the slogan \"Nika\" to demand the dismissal of unpopular ministers [PDF p. 102]. The mob refused to disperse and attempted to install Hypatius as emperor, exploiting the city\u2019s lack of troops [PDF p. 102]. Belisarius led the Imperial Guard in a counterattack, but the fighting caused a massive fire that destroyed St. Sophia and other buildings [PDF p. 103]. Hypatius was eventually forced into the Hippodrome and crowned by the insurgents [PDF p. 103]. While ministers urged Justinian to flee, the author notes that Empress Theodora refused to leave, arguing that imperial dignity was preferable to exile [PDF p. 105]. Spurred by her resolve, Justinian ordered a final assault on the Hippodrome [PDF p. 105]. Belisarius\u2019s troops breached the gates and slaughtered the rebels, resulting in approximately 35,000 deaths [PDF p. 106]. The author observes that despite this violence, the factions continued to riot for fifty years [PDF p. 106]. However, the text qualifies that they never again threatened the regime as severely as during the Nika Sedition [PDF p. 106]. The narrative concludes by highlighting the unique historical impact of this specific uprising compared to later disturbances [PDF p. 106]."}], "stats": {"input_tokens": 2388, "total_output_tokens": 372, "reasoning_output_tokens": 0, "tokens_per_second": 71.17185124507576, "time_to_first_token_seconds": 3.433405}}}}
